{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001 assumes a large service 'can enforce heterogeneous dependencies by fiat,' but this does not establish that it WILL. The problem asks whether decentralization is more resilient than a large service with 'strictly more total internal redundancy'; 'strictly more redundancy' can be homogeneous copies of the same design. The claim conflates capability with actuality, and the second countercondition (large-service shared design-spec flaw) is exactly the world the problem setup describes, yet it is merely listed as a countercondition rather than incorporated. Thus the claim's pro-large-service conclusion is unsupported under the problem as stated.","counterexample":"A large service with 3 redundant modules all implementing the same design spec has strictly more redundancy than 3 small services, yet fails together on a design-spec fault; SRC_001's 'can enforce' rationale does not apply, so its comparative resilience claim fails.","target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":false,"target_alias":"SRC_002"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_03's biological-genome analogy (more internal redundancy ~ more copies of the same genome; disease targets that genome) misrepresents the problem's term 'strictly more total internal redundancy,' which does not require identical-component copies; it can include heterogeneous design variants. The analogy's own overturn condition admits a large service could 'simulate diverse design philosophies,' contradicting the blanket transfer claim that strict redundancy is merely same-genome copies. Because the argument's comparative conclusion depends on this analogy, the argument fails the problem as stated.","counterexample":"A large service with two redundant modules using different OS stacks (e.g., Linux and seL4) has strictly more total internal redundancy and design heterogeneity, so the 'same genome / disease' analogy does not capture the class of large services the problem includes.","target_alias":"SRC_003"}]}